surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Wheatley</surname><given-names>AH</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Transpharyngeal approach for the treatment of dysphagia due to Forestier&#x02019;s disease</article-title><source><italic>Journal of Laryngology and Otology</italic></source><year>1999</year><volume>113</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>366</fpage><lpage>368</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10474677</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B19"><label>19</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Heeneman</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Vocal cord paralysis following approaches to the anterior cervical spine</article-title><source><italic>Laryngoscope</italic></source><year>1973</year><volume>83</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>17</fpage><lpage>21</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">4683910</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig id="fig1" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p> (a) a hard mass protruding in the posterior hypopharyngeal wall. (b) CT scan shows a bony mass pushing the posterior piriform sinus wall anteriorly. (c) Axial T1 weighted-MRI shows a mass pushing the posterior piriform sinus wall anteriorly. (d) sagittal T1 weighted-MRI shows an anterior mass of the cervical spine from C4 to T1.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CRIM.OTOLARYNGOLOGY2012-123825.001"/></fig></floats-group></article>